7.A case example applying the Restatement (Second) standard. Joyner v. Adams involved a dispute over the pricing provision in a real estate agreement. The parties’ dispute, in essence, turned on whether the phrase “all portions deemed lots” merely required the lessee/developer to partition the land into separable lots that were ready for construction or required that buildings actually be under construction on each lot. There were three rounds of litigation. During round one, the trial court apparently concluded that the disputed pricing provision was facially unambiguous and thus ruled on summary judgment in favor of the defendant. The plaintiffs appealed and the North Carolina Court of Appeals reversed, holding that the disputed contractual provision was actually ambiguous, and remanded “for a factual determination of the parties’ intent.” Joyner v. Adams, 361 S.E.2d 902, 902 (N.C. Ct. App. 1987).

Round two involved a bench trial (the parties apparently waived their right to a jury trial). However, the trial court made a mistake: The court applied only § 201(1) of the Restatement—did the parties share the same meaning of the pricing provision at formation? After concluding that the parties did not share the same meaning, the trial court should have applied §§ 201(2) and (3)—what did the parties know or have reason to know about each others’ understanding of the pricing provision at formation? But instead, the trial court applied the maxim “construe against the drafter” and ruled for the plaintiffs on that ground. (A comment below discusses many of the traditional maxims of contract interpretation that a court can use as part of the interpretive process.). This time, the defendant appealed and the North Carolina Court of Appeals reversed again. The appellate court upheld the trial court’s application of § 201(1) but reversed and remanded the case for another trial because the trial court skipped §§ 201(2) and (3). Id. at 902, 905-906.

Round three involved a second trial that focused on §§ 201(2) and (3). The trial court found that the defendant neither knew nor had reason to know of the plaintiff’s understanding of the disputed price provision. It therefore ruled for the defendant. The plaintiffs appealed again (as they had during round one). But this time, the North Carolina Court of Appeals affirmed the trial court. Joyner v. Adams, 387 S.E.2d 235, 238-239 (N.C. Ct. App. 1990).

8.Scholarly commentary. Professor Lisa Bernstein is skeptical about the weight that decision makers should give to general standards, such as trade usage. Professor Bernstein has drawn a distinction between cases in which the parties wish to preserve their relationship and “end game” situations in which the relationship has collapsed and the parties are attempting to maximize their gains or minimize their losses. She argues that norms such as trade usage, course of dealing, and course of performance are relationship preserving, but parties to a dispute do not usually want a dispute-resolution body to apply these norms, preferring that relationship-preserving norms be left to the nonlegal realm. Lisa Bernstein, Merchant Law in a Merchant Court: Rethinking the Code’s Search for Immanent Business Norms, 144 U. Pa. L. Rev. 1765, 1770-1771 (1996).

Other commentators have considered issues of contract interpretation from an economic perspective. Professors Alan Schwartz and Robert Scott have p. 414argued that courts, in interpreting written contracts between firms, should adopt a strict formalist approach rather than a contextual one because this is what firms themselves prefer. Alan Schwartz & Robert E. Scott, Contract Theory and the Limits of Contract Law, 113 Yale L. J. 541 (2003). Further discussion of this proposition can be found in James W. Bowers, Murphy’s Law and the Elementary Theory of Contract Interpretation: A Response to Schwartz and Scott, 57 Rutgers L. Rev. 587 (2005); Juliet P. Kostritsky, Plain Meaning v. Broad Interpretation: How the Risk of Opportunism Defeats a Unitary Default Rule for Interpretation, 96 Ky. L.J. 43 (2008); Steven J. Burton, A Lesson on Some Limits of Economic Analysis: Schwartz and Scott on Contract Interpretation, 88 Ind. L.J. 339 (2013).

Comment: Interpretive Principles

Over the years, courts have developed a number of principles of interpretation to aid them in giving meaning to expressions of contractual agreement. These principles must be distinguished from the various categories of interpretive evidence, such as preliminary negotiations and trade usage. The principles can be thought of as rules that courts apply to the various classes of interpretive evidence. Some principles instruct courts how to analyze various types of evidence. Others tell the courts when some type of evidence takes priority over other types. Some of the rules are mandatory. But most are merely recommendations that must be weighed against other rules and evidence. See Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 202, Comment a.

The various principles are often called “canons of construction” or “maxims of interpretation.” And some are typically identified by their Latin name. Note also that many of the rules overlap or are closely related. Ten of the most important rules are discussed in this comment.

1.Interpret the contract as a whole. All of the text in a written contract, or in a set of writings that constitute a single contract, should be interpreted together as a whole. In other words, every provision in the contract should be interpreted as part of a broader agreement and should not be construed in isolation from the rest of the language of the contract. See Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 202(2). Frequently, a word or sentence that is patently ambiguous in isolation is made clear when it is read in the context of the entire written agreement. For example, the word “bank” has multiple meanings, but in a financing agreement, it clearly means a place where one borrows money, not an area near a river. See also id. § 202, Comment d (“A word changes meaning when it becomes part of a sentence, the sentence when it becomes part of a paragraph. A longer writing similarly affects the paragraph[.]”). This principle of interpretation is mandatory in every American jurisdiction. Note that the next two principles are best thought of as subrules of this rule.

2.Avoid interpretations that result in terms conflicting. Put another way, an interpretation of a contract is preferred if it results in all of the provisions working together “in harmony.” Carter v. Cline, 385 S.W.3d 745, 752 (Ark. 2011); accord p. 415Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 202(5) (“Wherever reasonable, . . . [the terms of an] agreement are interpreted as consistent with each other[.]”).

3. Avoid interpretations that make terms superfluous. The Restatement (Second) provides that an interpretation under which all terms of the contract are “effective” is preferred to an interpretation under which part of the contract has “no effect.” Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 203(a). This means that an interpretation is preferred under which none of the terms are treated as superfluous, inoperative, or as if they do not exist. The policy underlying this principle is explained in § 203, Comment b: “Since an agreement is interpreted as a whole, it is assumed in the first instance that no part of it is superfluous. . . . Where an . . . agreement has been negotiated with care and in detail and has been expertly drafted for the particular transaction, an interpretation is very strongly negated if it would render some provisions superfluous.”

4.Reasonable meanings are preferred over unreasonable meanings. To elaborate, interpretations that do not make business sense or are otherwise illogical should be avoided. Put another way, any interpretation that would make the parties appear irrational should be avoided. An excellent example of this principle is contained in Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 203, Illustration 1: “A licenses B to manufacture pipes under A’s patents, and B agrees to pay ‘a royalty of 50 cents per 1,000 feet for an output of 5,000,000 or less feet per year, and for an output of over 5,000,000 feet per year at the rate of 30 cents per thousand feet.’ The 50 cent rate is payable on the first 5,000,000 feet, the 30 cent rate only on the excess. The more literal reading is unreasonable, since it would involve a smaller payment for 6,000,000 feet than for 4,000,000 feet.”

5.The purposes of the contract. The principal purposes of the parties in entering a contract are given considerable weight in determining the agreement’s meaning. See Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 202(1). Often principal purposes of a contract can be determined from the “recitals” or “whereas” clauses. Drafters of contracts must be aware of the importance of these provisions in case an issue should later develop about the interpretation of the contract. An excellent example of this principle in operation is Cragmere Holding Corp. v. Socony-Mobil Oil. Co., 167 A.2d 825 (N.J. Super. Ct. App. Div. 1961). There, plaintiff leased a gas station to defendant for a long term. Under the lease, the plaintiff/lessor promised that “no real property . . . within 1000 feet of the premises . . . owned or leased by the lessor shall be developed or used for the . . . sale or distribution of petroleum products.” Plaintiff later acquired some additional land within 1,000 feet of the premises and filed a lawsuit asking the court to declare that it was permitted to use the new land to sell oil and gasoline, despite the provision in the lease with the defendant/lessee quoted above. Plaintiff argued that “property . . . owned or leased by the lessor” as used in the contract only meant property owned or leased by plaintiff at the time the contract was formed. Thus, the lease did not restrict how plaintiff used the new land. Defendant countered that the contract covered all land within 1,000 feet of the premises, regardless of when it was acquired by plaintiff. Based on evidence submitted at stage two (the resolution of ambiguity), the court found that the purpose of the clause at issue was to bar plaintiff from competing with p. 416defendant within 1,000 feet of the leased premises. The court thus ruled for the defendant, finding that the competition restriction provision applied to all land within 1,000 feet, not just land owned by plaintiff when the contract was formed.

6.Noscitur a sociis. The meaning of a word is affected by its immediate context, such as by other words in the same series. See, e.g., Popkin v. Sec. Mut. Ins. Co., 367 N.Y.S.2d 492 (App. Div. 1975) (holding that “flood” in an insurance contract meant inundation from natural water sources, not damage from a broken water main, because the contract covered losses from “flood, surface waters, waves, tidal water or tidal wave, [and] overflow of streams or other bodies of water”; since all of the other items in the list concern natural phenomena, “flood” should be read in the same way). The next two principles are more specific versions of this principle.

7.Ejusdem generis. When a general term is combined with a specific one, the general term will be deemed to include only things that are of the same type as the specific term. For example, assume seller contracts to sell buyer his farm together with the “cattle, hogs, and other animals.” This probably would not include seller’s favorite house dog, but probably would include the sheep that seller was raising on the farm for the market. This example comes from Edwin Patterson, The Interpretation and Construction of Contracts, 64 Colum L. Rev. 833, 853 (1964).

8.Expressio unius exclusio alterius. “If one or more specific items are listed, without any more general or inclusive terms, other items, although similar in kind, are excluded.” Id. 853. For example, suppose seller contracts to sell buyer his farm together with “the cattle and hogs on the farm.” This language would be interpreted to exclude the seller’s favorite house dog and the sheep. Id. at 853-854.

9.Specific provisions are exceptions to and control contradictory general provisions. See Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 203(c) (“[S]pecific terms . . . are given greater weight than general language[.]”). This principle normally applies when a contract contains two inconsistent provisions—(1) a narrow term that covers a particular topic, and (2) a broad term that covers the same topic and various other topics. In such a contract, the specific term should be understood to qualify the general term—i.e., to override the general term as to the particular subject governed by the specific term.

To illustrate, in J.E. Faltin Motor Transp., Inc. v. Eazor Express, Inc., 273 F.2d 444 (3d Cir. 1960), a lease of a truck provided that the lessee should be absolutely liable for loss or damage to the vehicle. But another clause in the same contract stated that no party’s liability should be increased by the contract. In interpreting this contract, the court reasoned that the former clause was more specific than the latter provision. The first clause only concerned a particular type of liability—liability for loss or damage to the vehicle—making it specific. The second clause concerned every possible type of liability, making it broad. Thus, the court concluded that the former clause qualified (or overrode) the latter clause when it came to the topic of liability for loss or damage to the vehicle.

p. 417
Drafting contracts with inconsistent provisions is a bad practice. The better approach is to include additional language in the contract that explains how the terms relate so that they will not be seen as conflicting with each other. In other words, it is best to explain in the written contract itself (1) that the specific provision qualifies the general provision, and (2) how the qualification should be understood. Unfortunately (or fortunately for litigators), contracts with conflicting provisions are all too common. That arguably makes this principle one of the most useful rules of interpretation.

10.Contra proferentem—construe against the drafter. According to this maxim, if a writing contains language that is capable of two reasonable meanings, the interpretation that is less favorable to the party that drafted the contract shall be preferred. See Restatement (Second) of Contracts § 206; see also, e.g., St. Charles Foods, Inc. v. America’s Favorite Chicken Co., 198 F.3d 815 (11th Cir. 1999) (agreement between franchisor and franchisee that was ambiguous about scope of right of first refusal was construed against franchisor that drafted the language). Several points about this principle must be kept in mind.

First, most jurisdictions limit the use of contra proferentem to cases where one party can fairly be regarded as being solely responsible for drafting the contractual language in question. If the parties worked on the language together, the rule does not apply. Compare W. Sling & Cable Co. v. Hamilton, 545 So. 2d 29, 32 (Ala. 1989) (“[w]here both parties to a contract are sophisticated business persons advised by counsel and the contract is a product of negotiations at arm’s length between the parties, . . . no reason to automatically construe ambiguities in the contract against the drafter”), with Speedway Motorsports Int’l Ltd. v. Bronwen Energy Trading, Ltd., 707 S.E.2d 385 (N.C. Ct. App. 2011) (sophisticated parties, but language chosen by one; maxim applied). See generally Meredith R. Miller, Contract Law, Party Sophistication and the New Formalism, 75 Mo. L. Rev. 493 (2010) (suggesting caution in characterizing parties as “sophisticated,” to avoid exacerbating imbalances of bargaining power).

Second, “construe against the drafter” is “generally said to be a rule of last resort and is applied only when other . . . rules of interpretation have failed to elucidate the contract’s meaning.” 11 Williston on Contracts § 32:12, pp. 790-791 (4th ed. 2012); accord Cruz v. Visual Perceptions, LLC, 84 A.3d 828, 837 (Conn. 2014).

Third, there is one type of contract for which “construe against the drafter” plays a larger role: adhesion contracts. Adhesion contracts are contracts that meet the following three elements: they are (1) standard form contracts, (2) offered by a business to consumers, (3) on a take it or leave it basis with no opportunity for the consumer to negotiate terms. But construe against the drafter is not limited to such contracts. See, e.g., St. Charles Foods, Inc. v. America’s Favorite Chicken Co., 198 F.3d 815 (11th Cir. 1999) (letter agreement with franchisee that was ambiguous about right of first refusal construed against franchisor that drafted language); Guerrant v. Roth, 777 N.E.2d 499 (Ill. App. Ct. 2002) (ambiguity in contingency fee agreement written by attorney particularly subject to rule requiring construction against drafter).

